<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ page_language|default('en') }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover">
<title>{{ t("learn.page.learn-targum", "Learn — targum") }}</title>
{% include '_icons.html.j2' %}
<style>{{ asset('reader.css') }}{{ asset('chrome.css') }}{{ asset('words.css') }}{{ asset('library.css') }}{{ asset('learn.css') }}</style>
{{ hebrew_face() }}
<script>{{ asset('keep.js') }}</script>
{#- The key, before anything that reads it (targum-internal#343). `building.js`, and
    the conversation's pill, the followed series and the palette in the nav, each read `window.TARGUM_KEY`
    once as they load; it was set in a script at the foot of the body, so on a local serve
    they held an empty key for the life of the page and the bell, the followed series and
    the chat list were refused. The hosted box has no key and was never affected. -#}
<script>window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";</script>
</head>
{#- `words` as well as `learn`: the panels, the list controls, the word table and the
    status pips all come from words.css, and the pip colours (--step-1..4) are defined on
    `.words` itself. -#}
<body class="words learn">
{% set here = 'learn' %}
{% set title = '' %}
{% include '_nav.html.j2' %}
<script>{{ asset('building.js') }}</script>

<main id="page" hidden>
  {#- How much of the language you have. First thing on the page, and a real count of a
      real thing rather than a score — §6: no invented currency. The lists of the words
      and phrases themselves left this page on 2026-09-11 for Your Words, behind the
      account: Learn is the room you learn in — the text, the conversation, the shelf —
      and not a lobby. -#}
  <div class="front" id="front">
    {#- One row at the head of the desk (2026-09-11: "less empty space and more filled
        up by the reader"): the heading of the sheet at the start, the count at the end.
        On a phone the row comes after the sheet, which takes the screen. -#}
    {#- Decided with David on 2026-09-11 ("I definitely don't know what I'm looking
        at"): the page opens with a greeting and today — the time of day, the reader's
        name, the date and the week's portion — with the count of words at the end of
        the row; under it a row of doors, one press each, that swap the sheet: what you
        were reading, what is next, the week's portion, a cycle you follow. The reader
        stays first: one text is open the moment the page is. -#}
    {#- "Put the buttons together at the top, in a sleek design" (2026-09-11): the
        greeting at the start with today and the count on one line under it, and the
        row of doors as one control at the end — Continue reading, Suggested, your
        subscriptions. The conversation is the pill at the foot of the page. -#}
    <div class="learn-head">
      <div class="hello">
        <p class="greeting" id="greeting"></p>
        <p class="today-line"><span class="today" id="today"></span><span class="known-line" id="known-line"></span></p>
      </div>
      <div class="ways" id="doors" hidden></div>
    </div>
    {#- The sheet (§13, 2026-09-11): the text to carry on with, drawn as itself — the
        reader, framed and working, at the place it was left, on the desk, across the
        row; under it the title, what it is, and one press to the reader's own page. Learn
        is the reader's own highlight: the conversation is the pill at the foot of every
        page (2026-09-11). The frame is
        told it is on the front page (`preview=1`): it draws no bar, keeps its own links
        inside itself, sends every other link to this page, and counts a visit at the
        first real press rather than at being shown. The one pure-paper surface on the
        page, and the brightest thing on it: the memory the front page protects is the
        text with its English beside every line, so the front page shows one. -#}
    {#- What a new reader is asked (targum-internal#294, #306, #334): what they came for,
        then how much Hebrew they have — one question a screen, each with a Skip, and the
        last answer opens the text it chose. The rung was asked and thrown away
        (2026-09-17), then not asked (2026-09-18); since 2026-09-19 it is asked and *kept*,
        as a seed the reader's own marked words outvote. design.md §12 has the whole of
        it. The subjects are asked in a person's own words, three at least, and every one
        is offered whether or not the shelf can answer it yet — see `learn.js`.

        Shown once, to a reader who has opened nothing and answered nothing. At a desk it
        stands above the sheet, so the text is still there under it; on a phone there is
        no sheet, so it is the screen, with Next and Skip at its foot. Drawn and filled by
        `learn.js`. -#}
    <section class="arrival" id="arrival" hidden aria-labelledby="arrival-asks">
      <p class="arrival-step" id="arrival-step"></p>
      <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-subjects">
        <p class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks">{{ t("learn.arrival.what-are-you-interested-in", "What are you interested in?") }}</p>
        <p class="arrival-hint">{{ t("learn.arrival.pick-three-or-more", "Pick three or more. We use them to choose what to put in front of you.") }}</p>
        <div class="arrival-doors" id="arrival-doors" role="group" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks"></div>
      </div>
      <div class="arrival-screen" id="arrival-level" hidden>
        <p class="arrival-asks" id="arrival-asks-level">{{ t("learn.arrival.how-much-hebrew", "How much Hebrew do you have?") }}</p>
        <p class="arrival-hint">{{ t("learn.arrival.level-hint", "We start you there. What you mark as you go takes over from it.") }}</p>
        <div class="arrival-levels" id="arrival-levels" role="group" aria-labelledby="arrival-asks-level"></div>
      </div>
      <p class="arrival-foot">
        <button type="button" class="arrival-done" id="arrival-done" disabled>{{ t("learn.arrival.next", "Next") }}</button>
        <button type="button" class="arrival-skip" id="arrival-skip">{{ t("learn.arrival.skip", "Skip") }}</button>
        <span class="arrival-count" id="arrival-count" aria-live="polite"></span>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="carry-on" aria-labelledby="carry-heading">
      <div class="page-sheet" id="carry-sheet" hidden>
        <span class="card-cover" id="carry-cover" aria-hidden="true" hidden></span>
        {#- The head of the sheet, above the text (2026-09-11: "I definitely don't know
            what I'm looking at" with the title at the foot of a window the height of
            the screen): the title in its own face, what it is, how far along, and
            Open — so the first thing on the page says what the text is. -#}
        <div class="page-head">
          {#- How far through the text the reader is, as a line along the head's lower
              edge (2026-09-11); drawn only once something is finished. -#}
          <span class="page-progress" id="carry-progress" role="img" aria-label="" hidden></span>
          {#- What this text is to the reader — Continue reading, Up next, Start here,
              From the conversation — over the title. -#}
          <p class="page-state" id="carry-heading">{{ t("learn.page.continue-reading", "Continue") }}</p>
          <bdi class="page-title" id="carry-title"></bdi>
          <p class="page-facts">
            <span class="page-english" id="carry-english" lang="en" dir="ltr" hidden></span>
            <span class="card-track" id="carry-track" hidden></span>
            <span class="card-meta" id="carry-meta"></span>
            <span class="carry-known" id="carry-known" hidden></span>
          </p>
          {#- Reading in the sheet is comfortable, and the reader's own page is better
              (2026-09-14, David: "while they can comfortably read on that page ... for
              the best experience, they should click open and go to the reader page").
              Said once, beside the press, and only over a framed reader. -#}
          <p class="page-hint" id="carry-hint">{{ t("learn.page.read-here-or-go-full-screen", "Read here, or go full screen.") }}</p>
          <p class="page-actions">
            {#- Open goes to the reader's own page, and says so: "Open" alone read as
                opening something inside the sheet. The glyph is the expand corners a
                window is made full-size by. -#}
            <a class="open" id="carry" href="/"><svg class="open-glyph" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M9.5 2.5h4v4M13.5 2.5 9 7M6.5 13.5h-4v-4M2.5 13.5 7 9"/></svg><span>{{ t("learn.page.open-the-reader", "Open it") }}</span></a>
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="page-window" id="carry-window" hidden>
          <iframe class="page-frame" id="carry-frame" title=""></iframe>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    {#- On a phone, cards instead of the sheet (David, 2026-09-14): one for every text the
        page can offer, each a press to its reader. Drawn by `learn.js`, shown by the
        stylesheet under 40rem. -#}
    {#- What to work on (targum-internal#103), under the sheet rather than over it.

        Reading is what the front door is for, so the record comes after it: the words
        you flagged and never came back to are worth meeting, and they are not worth
        being asked about before you have read a line. Five here and the rest on Your
        Words — the same cap-and-see-all every other list on this page has had since
        it was written.

        Still pull and never push: no count beside the heading, nothing due, and a
        reader with nothing to work on sees no panel at all. -#}
    <section class="panel work front-work" id="work-on" hidden>
      <h2>{{ t("yours.page.what-to-work-on", "What to work on") }}</h2>
      {#- The third moment (targum-internal#335): the first time this fold has anything in
          it, it says what it is, once. A new reader marked a few words in a text ten
          minutes ago, and this is where they find out the page kept them. -#}
      <p class="note work-once" id="work-once" hidden>{{ t("learn.work.once", "These are the words you marked. We keep them here, and they'll be waiting whenever you come back.") }}</p>
      {#- Two halves, one question (2026-09-18): the words, and the phrases — the ones
          kept from a text and the lines the conversation corrected. The tabs are drawn
          only when both have rows; the door and the way to the rest follow the open one. -#}
      <div class="tabs work-tabs" id="work-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="{{ t("lists.work.words-or-phrases", "Words or phrases") }}" hidden>
        <button type="button" class="tab" role="tab" id="work-tab-words" data-work="words" aria-controls="work-rows" aria-selected="true">{{ t("lists.work.words", "Words") }}</button>
        <button type="button" class="tab" role="tab" id="work-tab-phrases" data-work="phrases" aria-controls="work-phrase-rows" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">{{ t("lists.work.phrases", "Phrases") }}</button>
      </div>
      <ul class="work-rows" id="work-rows" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="work-tab-words"></ul>
      <ul class="work-rows" id="work-phrase-rows" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="work-tab-phrases" hidden></ul>
      <p class="work-foot" id="work-foot" hidden>
        <button type="button" id="work-talk">{{ t("lists.work.talk-words", "Generate sentences with these words") }}</button>
        <button type="button" class="work-more" id="work-more" hidden><svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M13 8a5 5 0 1 1-1.6-3.7"/><path d="M13 2.5v2.6h-2.6"/></svg> {{ t("lists.work.more", "Show others") }}</button>
      </p>
      <a class="see-all" id="work-all" href="/words">{{ t("learn.page.all-your-words", "All your words") }}</a>
    </section>

    <section class="learn-cards" aria-label="{{ t("learn.page.your-texts", "Your texts") }}">
      <ul class="learn-card-list" id="learn-cards" hidden></ul>
      <a class="see-all" id="learn-cards-all" href="/texts" hidden>{{ t("learn.page.all-your-targums", "All your targums") }}</a>
    </section>

  </div>

  {#- Words you may already know, on the way in (targum-internal#297). The grid itself
      is #245's and lives on Your Words; this is the same grid offered at the one moment
      it is worth most — after somebody has read a text and watched a word land on their
      list, so they know what the list is for. Before that it is a vocabulary test.

      A reader at bet who answers one page of it moves from a handful of known words to
      a four-figure count, and the library re-sorts under them. Shown to a reader who has
      opened something and has few words; hidden for good once answered or waved away.
      Mounted by `learn.js`, which decides the moment. -#}
  <section class="panel claim claim-here" id="claim-here" hidden>
    <h2>{{ t("learn.claim.words-you-may-already-know", "Words you may already know") }}</h2>
    <p class="note">{{ t("learn.claim.if-you-ve-read-hebrew-before", "If you've read Hebrew before, tell us which of these you know and the library will sort itself to match.") }}</p>
    <div id="claim-here-body"></div>
    <p class="claim-away"><button type="button" id="claim-not-now">{{ t("learn.claim.not-now", "Not now") }}</button></p>
  </section>

  {#- The shelf that stood here left on 2026-09-11 ("removed from underneath"): the
      texts read lately are the Recently read menu in the row over the sheet, and the
      whole list, with its chapters and its trash, is Your targums (`/texts`). -#}
</main>

{#- The first thing a new reader sees, so it is the onboarding now. It points at the
    Library rather than at the upload form, which is the whole change. -#}
<section class="nothing" id="nothing" hidden>
  <p class="lede">{{ t("learn.page.nothing-here-yet", "Nothing here yet.") }}</p>
  <p>{{ t("learn.page.pick-something-from", "Pick something from") }} <a href="/library">{{ t("learn.page.library", "Library") }}</a>.</p>
  <p class="hint">{{ t("learn.page.or", "Or") }} <a href="/add">{{ t("learn.page.add-your-own", "add your own") }}</a>.</p>
</section>
{#- While the shelf is being asked for, and when it could not be (2026-09-14). The page
    stood blank under the bar until `/readers` answered, which on a large shelf is
    seconds; and a failed answer was told as "Nothing here yet", which is what a new
    account is told, not what a broken connection is. -#}
<p class="learn-waiting" id="learn-waiting" role="status">{{ t("learn.page.opening-your-texts", "Opening your texts…") }}</p>
<section class="nothing" id="learn-failed" role="alert" hidden>
  <p class="lede">{{ t("learn.page.we-couldn-t-load-your-texts", "We couldn't load your texts.") }}</p>
  <p>{{ t("learn.page.reload-the-page-to-try-again-everything", "Reload the page to try again. Everything you've read is still here.") }}</p>
</section>

{% include '_foot.html.j2' %}

<script>
window.TARGUM_KEY = "{{ token }}";
window.TARGUM_INTO = {{ into|tojson }};
window.TARGUM_LANGUAGES = { {% for code, name in languages %}"{{ code }}": "{{ name }}"{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} };
window.TARGUM_CATALOGUE = {{ catalogue|tojson }};
{% if strings %}window.TARGUM_STRINGS = {{ strings|tojson }};
{% endif %}</script>
<script>{{ asset('strings.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('charts.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('lang.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('sync.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('account.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('covers.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('shelf.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('scenes.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('claim.js') }}</script>
{#- The fold's own code, the same that draws it on Your Words. A second copy of it here
    would be a second answer to "what is worth going over", and two of those eventually
    disagree. `lists.js` draws only what it finds: this page holds the fold's markup and
    none of the table's, so none of the table is drawn. -#}
<script>{{ asset('vocab.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('lists.js') }}</script>
<script>{{ asset('learn.js') }}</script>
</body>
</html>
